WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:08.660
Here’s a story you might not have heard.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.969
In 1946, President Harry Truman created a
commission to make recommendations for how

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:15.879
to reform higher education.

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:21.000
After doing extensive research, the Commission
released a six volume report arguing that

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.570
higher education was becoming increasingly
important and should be accessible to EVERYONE

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:29.439
regardless of age, background, or ability
to pay.

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:34.410
As a first step, the Truman Commission proposed
making freshman and sophomore year of college

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.320
TOTALLY FREE at public universities.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.541
The Commission also recommended offering grants
to fully cover living expenses for every university

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:42.541
student who needed them.

00:00:42.541 --> 00:00:47.640
And it recommended providing all of this on
an INCLUSIVE BASIS: dismantling racial and

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.190
religious exclusion, increasing women’s
enrollment, and creating more flexible admission

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.609
standards to account for economic disadvantages.

00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:58.149
And that was all just supposed to be a first
step.

00:00:58.149 --> 00:00:59.149
How did they propose to pay for this?

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:05.210
The Commission found that “phenomenal increases
in productivity per worker” would generate

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:10.530
enough social surplus to “support education
at all levels far more adequately in the future

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:16.180
than we could in the past,” that is, so
long as we made education a collective priority.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.921
And then Congress implemented the Commission’s
recommendations, and college has been

00:01:19.921 --> 00:01:23.460
affordable to everybody who wanted to attend ever since.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.350
Okay, that last part isn’t true.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.140
Actually, it’s JUST THE OPPOSITE today.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.880
America is DROWNING in student debt – 1.7
TRILLION dollars of it, and growing.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.649
And it’s hard to IMAGINE a Presidential
Commission making the types of recommendations

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:42.520
that the Truman Commission did, even though
we live in a much wealthier, more integrated,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.869
and more technologically advanced society.

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:46.070
What happened?

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:51.201
Well, the Truman Commission was right that
productivity would continue to increase, but

00:01:51.201 --> 00:01:54.070
we did NOT make education a collective priority.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:58.909
Or, more accurately, POLITICAL ELITES who
were worried that too much EDUCATION might

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:02.520
lead to too much EQUALITY didn’t make it
a priority.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.999
So the GROWING WEALTH created by increased
productivity went toward making the rich richer,

00:02:06.999 --> 00:02:08.759
and not into educating more people.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:13.820
To see how this happened, we have to go back
to the end of the Second World War.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:17.709
That was the FIRST TIME many middle class
people started to go to college.

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:21.840
Because of a federal law called the GI Bill,
veterans – a huge percentage of the population

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.120
back then – could even attend college FOR
FREE.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.930
Because of state-level commitments to education,
public college was CHEAP and ACCESSIBLE for

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:33.209
massive numbers of people beyond veterans,
too.

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:37.430
There were still deep inequalities, but college
was no longer just a training ground for the

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.430
rich.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.930
With universities opening to more people,
feminists and the civil rights movement began

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.239
to break down barriers.

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:47.180
The increasing presence of working class people,
women, and people of color on college campuses

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.989
made them centers for political and social
dissent.

00:02:49.989 --> 00:02:53.970
Protest, debate, and activism became part
of college life.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.580
Soon there was a backlash.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:00.379
Some politicians thought this was TOO MUCH
free speech, from the WRONG type of people.

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:03.049
They wanted a return to the old system.

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:06.939
A candidate for California governor named
RONALD REAGAN started attacking people he

00:03:06.939 --> 00:03:09.530
called “CAMPUS RADICALS.”

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:14.659
In the year 1970, an adviser to Reagan declared
in a speech: “We are in danger of producing

00:03:14.659 --> 00:03:16.239
an educated proletariat.

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:17.500
That’s dynamite!

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.430
We have to be selective on who we allow to
go through higher education.”

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:25.500
When these politicians gained power, they
made sharp cuts to education funding.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.840
California, under Reagan’s influence, went
from funding 32 percent of the University

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.950
of California budget in 1974 to just 16 percent
in 2004.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:39.849
Elsewhere, cuts to education were justified
on the logic of “living within our means.”

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:43.379
President Gerald Ford criticized New York
City’s CUNY system for offering a program

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:48.329
where “any high school graduate, rich or
poor,” could attend without paying tuition.

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:49.329
Imagine that.

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:54.390
In the midst of a city budget crisis in 1976,
Wall Street bond investors forced CUNY to

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:56.170
charge tuition for the first time.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:00.769
While politicians were slashing funding, business
strategies to undermine working class power

00:04:00.769 --> 00:04:05.540
restructured industry in a way that redirected
income toward jobs in management and finance

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.040
and away from everyone else.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.950
The jobs that paid well required college degrees.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:14.310
That meant that, even as education funding
collapsed, the number of people entering college

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.360
continued to increase.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.750
So: incomes for most people were starting
to stagnate and higher education was becoming

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:25.970
more important in getting a job that paid
well, so more and more people were going to

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:26.970
college.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:31.460
Meanwhile, because of an ideology of false
scarcity, state funding was not keeping up.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:32.750
What filled the gap?

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:34.390
Student loans.

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:38.470
Student loans are actually a creation of the
federal government.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:43.000
They were created on the assumption that states
would mostly cover the cost of college, so

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.240
federal loans would be for a relatively small
number of people, paid off painlessly within

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:49.220
a few years of graduating.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:54.930
But as tuition climbed and state grants receded,
a whole INDUSTRY developed around student

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:55.930
lending.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:00.460
By the 1990s, massive student loans were the
norm, and colleges began to base their business

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:01.560
models around them.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.440
Fast forward to today, and the results have
been catastrophic.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.060
45 MILLION Americans have student loans, and
each graduating class owes more than the last.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.330
In 1980, there were only a few million dollars
of student loans.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.199
By 2000, that number was around 50 billion.

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:20.550
In 2010, 200 billion.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:25.220
And today, we have nearly TWO TRILLION dollars outstanding.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.400
Instead of starting a family or buying a car,
let alone starting a business or starting

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.610
a movement, young people today live with a
SWORD OF DEBT hanging over their head.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.300
Student debt is even causing many older people
to put off retirement.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:44.512
Things are so bad that one in fifteen student
borrowers has considered suicide because of it.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.830
Too many lives are being ruined.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.669
And here is the craziest part.

00:05:49.669 --> 00:05:52.740
All of that student debt DOESN’T HAVE TO
EXIST.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.880
I’m not saying that in a hypothetical way.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.150
I’m saying that if the President of the
United States wanted, he could wipe out almost

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.180
all student debt RIGHT NOW.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:06.620
You see, 95 percent of student debt is owed
to the federal Department of Education.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:10.889
And when Congress created the student loan
program, it gave the Secretary of Education

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:16.780
authority to “compromise, waive, or release”
or “modify” ANY federal student debt for

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:17.780
ANY reason.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.900
This is often called “settlement authority.”

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:23.540
Now, the settlement authority has never been
used to bring about broad cancellation.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:28.210
I’ve done a deep review of the relevant
case law, and the legal argument for it is

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.210
VERY strong.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.970
In fact, after I wrote a law review article
that made that case, a growing number of senators

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:37.199
have been convinced and began to advocate
for the Secretary of Education to cancel student

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:38.440
debt.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:42.509
Few things are certain in law, but clearly
the case is good enough that it’s at least

00:06:42.509 --> 00:06:43.680
worth a test.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.259
Think about it.

00:06:45.259 --> 00:06:49.050
Cancelling federal student loans would instantly
make tens of millions of people’s lives

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.050
better.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.700
Its benefits would flow to their families
and their communities, and help stimulate

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.160
the broader economy.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:07:00.370
But cancelling student debt is only the first
step toward building a higher education system

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:02.290
that works for all.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:07.070
If we care about living in a democratic society,
we should use our enormous wealth to invest

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:12.400
in making it possible for ANYONE to access
all the education they want.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.879
Certainly we should not condemn people to
a lifetime of debt for wanting to learn or

00:07:15.879 --> 00:07:17.280
better themselves.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:21.270
All of this was clear to a Presidential Commission
in 1946.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:25.500
It’s well past time to recover that vision
and to build on it.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:31.189
I’m Luke Herrine, PhD student at Yale Law
School, for the Gravel Institute.

